---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Searched from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Searched.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { md } from "../lib/md.js";
import raw from "../data/searched.json";
const REPO = "https://github.com/daviddef/TheFalco";

/* What has been read, page by page, and what was in it — including the years
   that held nothing. A null is a result: it is what stops the next person
   reading the same volume again.

   THE OUTCOME WORDS ARE THIS ARCHIVE'S OWN, and they are six because six
   different things happen to a search here:
     found         something new came back
     no Falco      the volume was read end to end and the family is not in it
     not there     the one record sought is not in a source that holds others
     nothing       the source holds nothing for this family at all
     blocked       the film is obstructed, or the volume was never digitised
     not searched  deliberately declined, with the reasoning set down
   «no Falco», «not there» and «nothing» are the nulls, and the count of them
   is the number this page exists to publish. */
/* The kit takes `what` and `got` as HTML the archive has already rendered and
   based with its own helpers, so they go through md() here rather than being
   escaped into literal asterisks and backticks. */
const rows = raw.map((r) => ({ ...r, what: md(r.what, u), got: md(r.got, u) }));
const NIL = ["no Falco", "not there", "nothing"];
const nils = raw.filter((r) => NIL.includes(r.outcome)).length;
const full = raw.filter((r) => (r.tags || []).includes("read in full")).length;
const imgs = raw.reduce((a, r) => a + (+(String(r.src).match(/(\d+) images/) || [])[1] || 0), 0);
---

<Base title="What Has Been Read"
      eyebrow="Coverage, including the years that held nothing"
      dek={`${rows.length} searches on the record, ${nils} of which came back empty. ${full} annual registers read page by page — ${imgs} images, some two thousand acts. This page exists so that a null is not lost: knowing a volume has been read and holds nothing is the difference between «we have not looked» and «it is not there».`}>

  <div class="card" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent)">
    <p><strong>Why record the negatives.</strong> A family archive that only publishes its finds
    invites the next person to repeat every search that failed. <strong>{nils} of the {rows.length}
    searches below returned nothing</strong>, and several of those nulls overturned or unsettled
    something this archive had been relying on. Every register read act by act is logged in the
    <a href={REPO + "/tree/main/notes"}>reading logs</a>, with each father's age, trade and street.</p>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.6rem"><strong>The outcome words are this archive's own, and
    they are not interchangeable.</strong> <em>No Falco</em> means a volume was read end to end and
    the family is not in it. <em>Not there</em> means the one record sought is missing from a source
    that holds others. <em>Nothing</em> means the source holds nothing for this family at all.
    <em>Blocked</em> means the film is obstructed or the volume was never digitised — which is not a
    negative, it is an obstacle. <em>Not searched</em> means the decision to skip it was taken
    deliberately and the reasoning is in the row.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Six more years, read a different way</h2>
  <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:1.2rem">
    <p><strong>The Arienzo death registers for 1809–1814</strong> — six years nobody had touched —
    were read on 13 September 2026 <strong>through their indexes rather than act by act</strong>. Each
    year is one to five images instead of about fifty, and each index lists every death of the year
    <strong>with both parents named</strong>.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:.6rem">They produced <strong>six Falco</strong>, including a daughter of
    generation two this archive did not have — <P n="Giovanna Falco" bold={true} />, died 15 December
    1812 — and the confirmation, from the 1851 index, that
    <P n="Gioacchino Falco" bold={true} /> was a son of <P n="Matteo Falco" bold={true} /> and
    <P n="Francesca Crisci" bold={true} />.</p>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:.6rem"><strong>Completeness, stated honestly:</strong> 1811,
    1813 and 1814 were read in full. For <strong>1809, 1810 and 1812 only some pages were read</strong>
    — in each case including the stretch where a <em>Matteo</em> would fall, since those indexes sort
    by forename. <a href={u("/method/")}>Why this replaced the act-by-act sweep →</a></p>
  </div>

  <h2>The register of what has been read</h2>
  <p class="dek">Filter by what the search returned, or by what it was about. Set down so nobody
  repeats them.</p>
  <Searched rows={rows} u={u} nil={NIL} unit="searches" />

  <h2>Still to read</h2>
  <p class="dek"><strong>The 1837–1843 run is finished.</strong> Seven consecutive volumes — 1837,
  1838, 1839, 1840, 1841, 1842, 1843 — have now been read act by act, end to end, together with
  1816–1828 and 1834. What remains is the <strong>birth registers before 1816 and after 1818</strong>,
  the <strong>Forchia birth register for 1897</strong> that nobody has indexed, and the
  <em>processetti</em> — <strong>258 volumes across four towns, of which none have been read</strong>.</p>
  <p class="small">One qualification on the table above, stated rather than hidden: for the 1816
  death register, images 3–39 were read in full <em>for Falco</em> — and the Falco answer is firm —
  but the act-by-act roster of the other families in that stretch was not written down at the time.
  Recovering it needs a second pass, and that pass is queued.</p>
  <p class="small">Everything above is in <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions</a> with what
  it would take to answer it.</p>
</Base>
